Synthesise BLK_STS_P2PDMA for member completions on kernels whose block layer
cannot produce it.

Upstream's v6 series teaches md that an unroutable PCI peer-to-peer transfer is
a property of the PCIe topology, not of device health: BLK_STS_P2PDMA fences the
range with a badblock instead of faulting the member or retrying. None of the
kernels this package ships to can emit that status:

  - 6.12 / 6.14 report BLK_STS_TARGET for an unroutable P2P write;
  - 6.17 / 7.0 report BLK_STS_INVAL, which md DELIBERATELY swallows since
    upstream f7b24c7b41f2 ("md/raid1,raid10: don't handle IO error for
    REQ_RAHEAD and REQ_NOWAIT") -- raid1_should_handle_error() returns false,
    the write is accounted successful, and the mirror diverges silently: one
    leg has the data, the other does not, and nothing records the difference.

So on every target kernel the upstream handling is dead code unless md is told
which completions really mean "no P2P path". This patch supplies that, in five
parts per personality, all gated on
`#if defined(HAVE_BLK_FEAT_PCI_P2PDMA) && !defined(HAVE_BLK_STS_P2PDMA)`.
The second half of the gate matters: on a kernel that DOES define the status,
translating INVAL or TARGET would be actively wrong -- there INVAL means a
genuinely invalid request and TARGET means NS_NOT_READY / LBA_RANGE /
CMD_INTERRUPTED, and mistranslating either would mis-fence a healthy member.

ACCEPTED TRADE on the kernels this patch DOES apply to. BLK_STS_TARGET means
NS_NOT_READY / LBA_RANGE / CMD_INTERRUPTED on those kernels too; on 6.12/6.14
it merely ALSO covers an unroutable P2P transfer, and the two are
indistinguishable at this layer. So on a P2P-advertising array a TAGGED bio
that fails with a genuine TARGET is translated to BLK_STS_P2PDMA, which makes
raid1_write_error() early-return -- no WantReplacement, no failfast md_error()
-- and, via part 4, fences the whole r1_bio range with rdev_set_badblocks()
instead of narrowing it down. Net: a genuinely sick member of a GDS array gets
fenced range-wide and is not escalated for replacement. That is the mandated
design, not a bug: it errs toward data integrity, the alternative being to
retry a transfer that cannot route and diverge the mirror silently.

1. TAG (raid1.h / raid10.h enum + raid1.c / raid10.c request functions).
   A new R1BIO_P2PDMA / R10BIO_P2PDMA state bit, set beside md_account_bio()
   in raid1_read_request(), raid1_write_request() and the raid10 twins. It has
   to be a state bit, and it has to be set at submit: md_bio_is_p2pdma() reads
   the bio's first bvec, and by completion bi_iter.bi_size has been consumed to
   0 so bio_has_data() is false and the pages are no longer visible. raid10's
   read-retry path resets r10_bio->state = 0 before re-entering
   raid10_read_request(), which re-sets the tag, so the reset is covered.
   The bit is inserted mid-enum (between WriteError and FailFast / FailFast and
   Discard) deliberately: the enum tail differs between the p2pdma pin and the
   composed meshstor-main tree, which appends R1BIO_Probe / R10BIO_Probe.
   r1_bio->state is in-memory only, so renumbering the later bits is harmless.

2. INBOUND TRANSLATION (all four *_end_*_request handlers). On a tagged bio,
   BLK_STS_INVAL and BLK_STS_TARGET are rewritten to BLK_STS_P2PDMA before any
   status is inspected, and upstream's own handling then runs unmodified.
   In the two WRITE endios this cannot simply be prepended: ignore_error is
   computed in its declaration initializer, and that initializer calls
   raid1_should_handle_error(), which is exactly the function that swallows
   INVAL. The declaration is therefore split from its assignment (a
   behaviour-neutral change on every kernel) and the translation goes between
   the two. The READ endios compute `int uptodate = !bio->bi_status`, whose
   truthiness the translation preserves -- all three statuses are nonzero -- so
   there it only needs to precede the if-chain.
   The raid10 read-endio hunk deliberately carries minimal symmetric context
   (two leading, two trailing anchor lines). In composed trees md-latency-ewma
   inserts a fold-kick block a few lines below, inside the same `uptodate`
   branch; generous context would anchor on shape-specific lines and reject at
   --fuzz=0 against whichever composition the patch was not cut against.

3. OUTBOUND CLAMP (call_bio_endio() in raid1, raid_end_bio_io() in raid10).
   A residual BLK_STS_P2PDMA on the MASTER bio becomes BLK_STS_IOERR. On a
   kernel that does not define the status, 18 is a zero-filled hole in
   blk_errors[] that still passes the ARRAY_SIZE bounds check, so
   blk_status_to_errno(18) returns 0 -- SUCCESS -- for a failed I/O. The value
   must never reach the O_DIRECT errno conversion.

4. TAG-GATED WRITE DISPATCH (handle_write_finished() / handle_write_completed()).
   Upstream's direct rdev_set_badblocks() arm is taken whenever the TAG is set,
   whatever the status, so a tagged bio never reaches narrow_write_error().
   The IsSync/IsRecover branch and the raid10 replacement-leg arms stay OUTSIDE
   the gate: replacement writes already go to md_error() at endio and can never
   reach narrow_write_error(), and the sync branch uses raw status booleans and
   already badblocks correctly.

5. narrow_write_error() STATUS CHECK. After submit_bio_wait(), a wbio whose
   bi_status is BLK_STS_P2PDMA is treated as failed rather than trusting the
   returned errno. narrow_write_error() re-submits chunks of the master bio
   straight to the member, so a chunk covering P2P pages can come back with a
   native BLK_STS_P2PDMA; blk_status_to_errno(18) is 0 here (blk_errors[18] is
   a zero-filled hole), so without this check the chunk is recorded as written
   and the range is never fenced. The emitter is the sibling
   meshstor-nvme-rdma package, which carries upstream v6 p9-p11 ("nvme-rdma:
   return BLK_STS_P2PDMA for unsupported P2P transfers" and its siblings) and
   produces the status for a real unroutable P2P transfer, so this check is
   live, not merely defensive.

   KNOWN RESIDUAL, deliberately NOT closed by this part: an UNTAGGED
   mixed-pgmap bio -- host page in bi_io_vec[0], P2P pages after, which
   md_bio_is_p2pdma() cannot detect because it only inspects the first bvec --
   is not covered.

   CORRECTED 2026-08-12. An earlier revision of this note said that on
   6.17/7.0 such a bio fails BLK_STS_INVAL and raid1_should_handle_error()
   swallows it. That mechanism is not reachable: since 6.17, bio_add_page()
   refuses a page whose pgmap does not match the previous bvec's (upstream
   requires it because blk_dma_map_iter_start() caches the P2PDMA mapping
   state from the first segment), so the bio cannot be BUILT and no
   completion status ever arises. On those kernels the residual does not
   exist at all -- verified by running md_p2p_test mixed=1 on 7.0, where the
   device-page add is refused and no I/O is submitted. The 6.12/6.14 clause
   was and remains correct: there the bio IS constructible, fails
   BLK_STS_TARGET, which maps to a nonzero errno, so the ordinary path
   already fences it. The design spec accepts this residual: it is bounded
   to crafted iovecs, and cuFile does not build them.

   The correction does not change what this patch does, only why. It does
   change the testing: because the scenario is unconstructible on the Ubuntu
   targets, hunk 5 had NO asserting coverage there, and its only nominated
   test (test_mixed_pgmap_untagged.sh) asserts nothing anywhere. Coverage is
   now carried by three selftests instead:
     - test_kernel_rejects_mixed_pgmap.sh   asserts which rule this kernel
       enforces, with host+host / dev+dev controls, so the answer is a
       measurement and not an inference from a failed I/O;
     - test_mixed_pgmap_untagged.sh         now SKIPs where the bio cannot be
       built (it previously PASSED there, having silently interpreted a leg
       that was never written);
     - test_narrow_write_error_untagged.sh  reaches hunk 5 directly on every
       target via an ORDINARY untagged write against a dm-errstat leg armed
       "5,p2pdma" -- no mixed-pgmap bio and no real P2P hardware needed.
       Verified to FAIL with hunk 5 removed (badblocks cover only part of the
       retried range) and PASS with it present.

Deliberately NOT changed: the raid1_p2pdma_observe() diagnostics latch still
gates on whether the array advertises, not on the new tag. Rewiring it here
would make diagnostics behave differently on kernels that have BLK_STS_P2PDMA
than on kernels that do not; a consistent approximation is preferable. The
translation does run ahead of the latch, so a tagged legacy failure is now
latched as a P2P verdict on every kernel -- which is the consistent answer.

Source-level, not a header shim: it adds preprocessor structure to drivers/md,
splits two declaration initializers, and introduces a state bit.

Hand-maintained: the `.when` guard (raid1-10.c:raid1_write_error) makes this
patch composition-specific, and regenerate.sh never re-diffs guarded patches.
Drift must be fixed by hand, keeping the raid10 read-endio hunk's context
minimal and symmetric.

